.docname {Knowledge & Assistance}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Inquiry | One question from one person | "Is Lab 2 too hot?" |
    | Consultation | Looking things up to answer one Inquiry | Reading Lab 2's temperature and limit |
    | Source | Something that can be looked at | A guide page, a live value |
    | Answer | The reply to one Inquiry | "Yes, 27 °C, limit 26" |
    | Citation | A pointer from a claim to its Source | "Live reading, 14:05" |
    | Pass | Statement of who you are and your memberships | Sam's pass lists Northfield |
    | Grounded | Backed by a Source that was really looked at | "27 °C", from a live reading |

**Question:** can someone explain all this to me? **Kind:** Supporting. **Needs:** [Identity & Access](domain-identity.qd), [Building Management](domain-buildings.qd), [Telemetry Ingestion](domain-ingestion.qd).

Ask in plain words, get an answer you can check. The rule above all: a wrong answer is worse than no answer.

---

## In the real world

Sam, walking down a corridor: "is the west wing still too hot?"

- Sam does not want to go looking for it.
- Sam will act on the answer, so it must be checkable.
- If the assistant does not know, it has to say so.

---

## The model

.mermaid
    classDiagram
        class Inquiry {
            person
            question
        }
        class Answer {
            text
            confidence
        }
        class Citation {
            excerpt
        }
        class Source {
            title
            content
        }
        Inquiry "1" *-- "1" Answer
        Answer "1" *-- "many" Citation
        Citation "many" ..> "1" Source : points to

An Answer means nothing without its Inquiry, so they live together. A Source is maintained on its own, so it stands apart.

---

## Answering

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        A["Inquiry arrives, with the pass"] --> B["Keep only what this person may see"]
        B --> C["Look at Sources"]
        C --> D{"Enough found?"}
        D -- "yes" --> E["Answer, a citation on each claim"]
        D -- "some" --> F["Partial answer, says what is uncertain"]
        D -- "no" --> G["I don't know"]

The filter comes **first**. What the assistant cannot read, it cannot leak (AI-4).

---

## Three answers

| Sam asks | What was found | Answer |
|---|---|---|
| Is Lab 2 too hot? | Reading 27 °C, limit 26 | "Yes, 27 °C, above the 26 °C limit." With citations |
| Why is Lab 2 hot? | Reading only, no cause | "It is 27 °C. I can't tell why." |
| Is Harbour Tower OK? | Nothing, Sam has no access | "I don't know." |

The third answer does not say "you have no access". Even that would confirm the building exists.

---

## Rules

1. Every claim has a citation. No citation, no claim (AI-2).
2. A citation can only point to something actually looked at.
3. "I don't know" is a normal answer, not a failure (AI-5).
4. Partial evidence means saying so, not sounding sure (AI-5).
5. Only data from the person's own organizations (AI-4).
6. Every Inquiry gets an Answer, even a confusing one.
7. Questions about now use current values (AI-3).
8. One Inquiry, one Answer. A follow-up is a new Inquiry.

---

## What it does not own

Nothing here is a fact about the world. Buildings stay in [Building Management](domain-buildings.qd), readings stay in [Telemetry Ingestion](domain-ingestion.qd). This domain asks them, changes nothing, and turns the answer into its own words.

Nobody depends on this domain.

---

## Open questions

- Should it remember earlier questions in a conversation?
- Should it ever do things, or only answer? Today, only answer.
- Two Sources disagree: which wins?
